            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

The air leakage is quite low for a house of this age. It was tested to be 3.9 air changes per hour at 50 Pa (ACH @ 50Pa) which is the standard measure for air leakage. Anything below 3 ACH starts to require a mechanical ventilation system to provide fresh air. There is not a significant savings for reducing airleakage in your house, only to stop drafts anywhere that it's a problem.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

Since your house is quite tight, there is no significant savings to air sealing without a ventilation system.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>The attic of your house was assumed to have 10 inches (R32) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 6 inches (R20) fiberglass batt insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 240&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 125 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;

</ceilings>
                <exposedFloors>The overhang at the front of the house appears to be 2x10 joists with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation.

&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding additional insulation you could potentially make the floor a little more comfortable but it wonÂt have a very large energy savings. The overhang can be insulated either on the exterior with foam insulation or from the inside with fiberglass batt.

</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 4 inches of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modeled replacing the insulation with 4 inches (R20) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene, insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 4 inches (R20) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the basement walls you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 180&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>If you are looking into replacing your propane hot water heater you could consider an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 130&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DWHR 



Drain water heat recovery systems can capture heat from showers and put that heat back into your hot water tank. The waste water is not reused, but the heat from it is collected by the cold water coming in. These systems are typically a copper pipe with a smaller copper coil wrapped around it. It is placed into a vertical section of the drain stack and is a passive way to save energy.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a drain water heat recovery pipe with an efficiency of at least 30%, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 65&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 700 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
